1.  **`test_msgq_full` setup:**
    ```c
    ZTEST(msgq_api_1cpu, test_msgq_full)
    {
        // ...
        k_msgq_init(&msgq1, tbuffer1, MSG_SIZE, 1); // Initializes msgq1 with capacity 1
        // ...
        ret = k_msgq_put(&msgq1, &data[0], K_NO_WAIT); // Puts one message, making the queue full
        zassert_equal(ret, 0); // This assertion must pass for the test to continue
        // ...
        tids[0] = k_thread_create(..., put_full_entry, &msgq1, ...); // Creates a thread to run put_full_entry
        // ...
    }
    ```
    At the point `put_full_entry` starts, `msgq1` is a message queue of capacity 1, and it contains 1 message. Thus, it is full.

2.  **`put_full_entry` logic:**
    ```c
    static void put_full_entry(void *p1, void *p2, void *p3)
    {
        int ret;
        /* make sure the queue is full */
        zassert_equal(k_msgq_num_free_get(p1), 0); // Asserts 0 free messages
        zassert_equal(k_msgq_num_used_get(p1), 1); // Asserts 1 used message (queue capacity is 1)

        ret = k_msgq_put(p1, &data[1], K_NO_WAIT); // Attempts to put another message with K_NO_WAIT
        zassert_equal(ret, -ENOMSG, "Put message to full queue"); // THIS IS THE FAILING ASSERTION (line 240)

        /* blocked to TIMEOUT */
        ret = k_msgq_put(p1, &data[1], TIMEOUT);
        zassert_equal(ret, -EAGAIN, "Put message to full queue"); // This assertion is for timed wait
        // ...
    }
    ```

According to the Zephyr `k_msgq_put` API documentation and source code, if a message queue is full and `k_msgq_put` is called with `K_NO_WAIT`, it should return `-ENOMSG`. The preceding `zassert_equal` calls confirm that the queue is indeed full (`used_msgs == 1`, `max_msgs == 1`).

The error log `(ret not equal to -ENOMSG)` indicates that `ret` was some value other than `-ENOMSG`.
If `ret` was `0`, it would imply that `k_msgq_put` succeeded in adding a message to a full queue, which would be an overflow and a critical bug in the kernel's message queue implementation.
If `ret` was `-EAGAIN`, it would imply that `K_NO_WAIT` was treated as a timed wait that expired, which is also incorrect behavior for `K_NO_WAIT`.

